userContent.text:
Build an interactive figure using SVG.js.

CONCEPT: When light inside a denser medium strikes a boundary, part reflects and part refracts away from the normal; as the incident angle grows the refracted ray swings toward the surface until, at the critical angle where sin(θc) = n₂/n₁, it grazes along the boundary and beyond that angle no light escapes — total internal reflection.
KEY INSIGHT (what students should walk away understanding): There is one sharp angle, fixed only by the ratio of the two indices (sin θc = n₂/n₁), past which no refracted ray exists at all and the light is completely trapped in the denser medium.
DEMO STEPS: 5 guided steps — implement the step player in #ui

FULL BLUEPRINT:
{
  "concept": "When light inside a denser medium strikes a boundary, part reflects and part refracts away from the normal; as the incident angle grows the refracted ray swings toward the surface until, at the critical angle where sin(θc) = n₂/n₁, it grazes along the boundary and beyond that angle no light escapes — total internal reflection.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal boundary line across the middle of the canvas separating the two media",
    "Lower region tinted pale blue, representing the denser medium, labeled 'n₁'",
    "Upper region left white/unshaded, representing the less dense medium, labeled 'n₂'",
    "Vertical dashed gray normal line through the point of incidence, extending above and below the boundary",
    "Solid incident ray with arrowhead travelling up-and-right inside the lower medium to the point of incidence, labeled 'Incident ray'",
    "Angle arc with label 'θ₁' between the incident ray and the normal, below the boundary on the left",
    "Solid reflected ray with arrowhead leaving the point of incidence down-and-right into the lower medium, labeled 'Reflected ray'",
    "Angle arc with label 'θ₁' between the reflected ray and the normal, below the boundary on the right",
    "Solid refracted ray with arrowhead leaving into the upper medium, bent farther from the normal, labeled 'Refracted ray'",
    "Angle arc with label 'θ₂' between the refracted ray and the normal, above the boundary",
    "Optional dashed reference ray drawn at the critical angle inside the lower medium with arc labeled 'θc' (interactive overlay, shown only when its toggle is on)",
    "Text annotation 'Total internal reflection' placed in the lower medium when θ₁ exceeds θc (interactive state, replaces the missing refracted ray)",
    "Small numeric readout of θ₁, θ₂ (or 'none'), θc and n₂/n₁ in a corner (interactive overlay, not part of the printed figure)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "incidentAngle",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Incident angle θ₁ (degrees)",
      "range": [
        0,
        89,
        1
      ],
      "default": 30,
      "teaches": "Increasing θ₁ swings the refracted ray farther from the normal until it flattens onto the boundary and then disappears entirely, while the reflected ray always remains — the critical angle is a sharp threshold, not a gradual fade."
    },
    {
      "id": "indexRatio",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Index ratio n₂/n₁",
      "range": [
        0.3,
        0.99,
        0.01
      ],
      "default": 0.66,
      "teaches": "The critical angle depends only on the two media through sin(θc) = n₂/n₁, so a larger index contrast traps light at much smaller incident angles."
    },
    {
      "id": "showCritical",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Show critical-angle reference ray",
      "default": false,
